<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What the default example really says</h2>
    <p>The calculator starts with a $300,000 balance at 6.5% and 28 years left, with $500 a month to put toward either the mortgage or investments. Paying extra clears the loan in 203 months, about 17 years, and saves $157,068 in interest. Investing the $500 at an assumed 7% instead ends $16,533 ahead at the original payoff date.</p>
    <p>That win depends on earning more than 6.70% a year for 28 years. The margin is only 0.3 percentage points, and the outcomes aren't symmetric:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Average investment return</th><th>Result vs. paying off early</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>8%</td><td>Investing ahead by $80,846</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7% (default)</td><td>Investing ahead by $16,533</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6%</td><td>Paying off ahead by $33,548</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5%</td><td>Paying off ahead by $72,108</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A 1% tax drag on a taxable account has the same effect as a 1-point lower return: at 7% with 1% drag, paying off early wins by $33,548.</p>

    <h2>The mortgage rate decides most of the answer</h2>
    <p>Holding the 7% return assumption, the same $500 a month gives opposite answers depending on the loan:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>3% mortgage:</strong> investing ends $175,864 ahead.</li>
      <li><strong>4% mortgage:</strong> investing ends $138,012 ahead.</li>
      <li><strong>7.5% mortgage:</strong> paying off early ends $44,081 ahead, with no market risk.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Low-rate mortgages from earlier years are cheap money, and prepaying them gives up a lot of expected return. At today's higher rates, the guaranteed return from prepaying is much harder to beat.</p>

    <h2>What the calculator can't weigh for you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Liquidity.</strong> Money in investments can be sold in an emergency. Money paid into the house can come back out only by selling or borrowing against it, and lenders tighten up exactly when people need cash.</li>
      <li><strong>Higher-return steps that usually come first.</strong> Capturing a full 401(k) employer match is an immediate return that neither option here can match, and paying off credit card debt at 20% or more beats both. An emergency fund protects against having to sell investments at a bad time.</li>
      <li><strong>Risk you can live with.</strong> A paid-off house lowers the income you need every month, which has value in a job loss or in retirement. The calculator measures net worth, not peace of mind.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
